Retry login writes that conflict with a concurrent login

Five methods on keystone's login path read a row and write it in an
outermost session_for_write of their own, and none of them is retried:
`set_last_active_at` (the first login of a UTC day, password and
federated), `add_user_to_group_expires` (once per mapped group of a
federated login) and `cleanup_stale_group_memberships` (a federated
login after the IdP dropped a group) in
keystone/identity/shadow_backends/sql.py, and `_record_failed_auth`
(every wrong password) and `_reset_failed_auth` (the first good login
after a wrong one) in keystone/identity/backends/sql.py. Since the
mariadb-operator chart 26.10.2 (C5C3/cobaltcore#1243) a fresh database
of CobaltCore OpenStack runs MariaDB 12.3.3 with
innodb_snapshot_isolation=ON, and the second of two such transactions
of one user fails with error 1020 where MariaDB 11.4 let it wait and
overwrite. oslo.db raises `DBConsistencyError`, a subclass of
`DBDeadlock`, and POST /v3/auth/tokens answers 500, for example with
"Record has changed since last read in table 'user'; try restarting
transaction" on the UPDATE of user.last_active_at. The
keystone-pressure job primes its token pool with eight concurrent
logins and stopped on that 500 in 7 of 16 runs of
https://github.com/C5C3/cobaltcore/actions/runs/43608252970 and in 11
of 16 of https://github.com/C5C3/cobaltcore/actions/runs/43465529953.

Measured on 2026-11-15 in a Docker rig (mariadb:12.3.3 and the
published ghcr.io/c5c3/keystone:2026.1, keystone 29.0.0 with oslo.db
18.0.0) in which 8 threads call one backend method for one user at the
same time, 20 rounds, 160 calls per method; the rig and the probe are
in C5C3/cobaltcore#1739. Without the patch:

- set_last_active_at: 121 of 160 calls fail, in 20 of 20 rounds.
- _record_failed_auth: 105 fail, and 55 of 160 failed logins are
  counted.
- _reset_failed_auth: 34 fail, in 12 of 20 rounds.
- add_user_to_group_expires: 80 fail in 20 of 20 rounds when it renews
  a membership, 78 in 19 of 20 when it adds a group again after a
  removal.
- cleanup_stale_group_memberships: 76 fail, in 20 of 20 rounds.

Every failure is `DBConsistencyError` with MariaDB error 1020. The
published 2026.2 image (keystone 30.0.0, oslo.db 18.1.1) fails the
first three the same way (76, 93 and 49 of 160). With the patch no call
fails and 160 of 160 failed logins are counted; the slowest call of
the first three takes 0.14 s, 0.56 s and 0.35 s, and with 32 threads 0
of 640 calls fail in each of them. On MariaDB 11.4.13
(innodb_snapshot_isolation=OFF) no call fails without the patch, and
100 of 160 concurrent failed logins are not counted: the old server
lost the update that the new one refuses.

The patch adds `retry_on_write_conflict` to keystone/common/sql/core.py,
one oslo_db.api.wrap_db_retry instance with retry_on_deadlock=True, and
puts it as the outermost decorator on the five methods, which change
nothing else. keystone/common/sql/__init__.py re-exports core.py with
`import *`, so both backends reach it as `sql.retry_on_write_conflict`.
Each method opens its own outermost transaction, so a retry starts a
new one with a new snapshot and reads what the other login committed:
set_last_active_at and _reset_failed_auth find the value written and
write nothing, _record_failed_auth increments the committed count, so
every failed login is counted, add_user_to_group_expires renews the
membership the other login added and returns False, and
cleanup_stale_group_memberships finds no stale row and returns []. The
login that won already reported the change, so shadow_federated_user
still invalidates the user's cache once.

The first wait is 0.05 s, each later one a random time below a bound
that doubles from 0.1 s and is capped at 1 s, so the ten retries of
WRITE_CONFLICT_MAX_RETRIES wait 0.05 + 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.4 + 0.8 + 5 x 1 =
6.55 s at most. oslo.db's default first wait is 1 s: with plain
wrap_db_retry(retry_on_deadlock=True) the slowest call of the first
three methods took 1.09 s, 3.19 s and 1.24 s, and the keystone-operator
runs 2 uWSGI processes with 1 thread per pod by default, so a waiting
login blocks half a pod. The retry takes `DBDeadlock` (MySQL errors
1213 and 1205) and its subclass `DBConsistencyError` (1020);
`DBConnectionError`, `DBDuplicateEntry` and every other exception leave
on their first occurrence. A retry is logged at DEBUG only ("Performing
DB retry for function ..."), because a collision on the first logins of
a day is normal operation. When the retries run out, oslo.db logs "DB
exceeded retry limit." with the traceback at ERROR and re-raises, and
the Flask handler of patch 0003 answers a JSON 500. The count and the
intervals are constants, like the arguments of upstream's own
wrap_db_retry uses.

On a server that does not raise the conflict (MariaDB 11.4, or
innodb_snapshot_isolation=OFF) concurrent failed logins are still
under-counted, as before: the retry runs only on an error.

Tests: no upstream test moves. No existing test asserts that a
conflict escapes or how many transactions a method opens. The patch
adds `conflicting_writes` to keystone/tests/unit/utils.py, a context
manager that fails the first write transactions when they end, after
the method's real body ran, and counts the transactions opened. Its
`winner` argument commits the other login's write in a transaction of
its own after the failure and before the retry. It adds twelve tests:

- ShadowUsersBackendTests
  (keystone/tests/unit/identity/shadow_users/test_backend.py, run by
  ShadowUsersTests and its subclasses in
  keystone/tests/unit/test_shadow_users.py):
  test_set_last_active_at_retries_a_conflicting_write,
  test_set_last_active_at_retries_a_changed_record (DBConsistencyError),
  test_set_last_active_at_raises_after_the_last_retry (11 transactions,
  waits of 0.05, 0.1, 0.2, 0.4 and 0.8 s and five of 1 s when every
  random wait takes the top of its range, the row unchanged) and
  test_set_last_active_at_does_not_retry_a_lost_connection.
- LockingOutUserTests
  (keystone/tests/unit/identity/test_backend_sql.py):
  test_failed_auth_is_counted_once_after_a_conflicting_write,
  test_concurrent_failed_logins_are_both_counted (the other login
  counts its failure first, and the retry counts 2) and
  test_failed_auth_reset_retries_a_conflicting_write.
- FederatedGroupCacheTest
  (keystone/tests/unit/test_user_group_cache_invalidation.py):
  test_new_membership_retries_a_conflicting_write (True, one row),
  test_membership_added_by_a_concurrent_login_is_renewed (the other
  login adds the membership first: False, one row),
  test_expiring_membership_renewal_retries_a_conflicting_write,
  test_stale_membership_cleanup_retries_a_conflicting_write, which also
  asserts one transaction and [] for a user without memberships, and
  test_membership_removed_by_a_concurrent_login_is_not_reported (the
  other login removes the membership first: [], no row).

With only the test hunks applied, all but
test_set_last_active_at_does_not_retry_a_lost_connection fail.

Proof: test_login_write_retry_patch_applied in
tests/container-images/verify_keystone.sh makes the first write
transaction of each of the five methods raise `DBDeadlock` in the built
image and asserts that the method opens a second one.

The seven files carry the same blobs in keystone 29.0.0 and 30.0.0, and
the twins under patches/keystone/2026.1/ and patches/keystone/2026.2/
differ only in their Applies-to line.

Applies to keystone 29.0.0 (2026.1).

Upstream status: not yet proposed.

diff --git a/keystone/common/sql/core.py b/keystone/common/sql/core.py
index 2e34830..b01e6ed 100644
--- a/keystone/common/sql/core.py
+++ b/keystone/common/sql/core.py
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@ CONF() because it sets up configuration options.
 import datetime
 import functools
 
+from oslo_db import api as oslo_db_api
 from oslo_db import exception as db_exception
 from oslo_db import options as db_options
 from oslo_db.sqlalchemy import enginefacade
@@ -640,3 +641,23 @@ def handle_conflicts(conflict_type='object'):
         return wrapper
 
     return decorator
+
+
+# Retries a write transaction that lost against a concurrent one:
+# DBDeadlock (MySQL errors 1213 and 1205) and its subclass
+# DBConsistencyError (MariaDB error 1020, "Record has changed since last
+# read", under innodb_snapshot_isolation=ON). Every other error leaves on
+# its first occurrence. The first wait is 0.05 s, each later one a random
+# time below a bound that doubles from 0.1 s and is capped at 1 s, so ten
+# retries wait at most 0.05 + 0.1 + 0.2 + 0.4 + 0.8 + 5 x 1 = 6.55 s.
+# Decorate a method whose outermost transaction is its own, so that a
+# retry starts a new transaction that reads the committed rows.
+WRITE_CONFLICT_MAX_RETRIES = 10
+
+retry_on_write_conflict = oslo_db_api.wrap_db_retry(
+    retry_interval=0.05,
+    max_retries=WRITE_CONFLICT_MAX_RETRIES,
+    inc_retry_interval=True,
+    max_retry_interval=1,
+    retry_on_deadlock=True,
+)
